    "font" : "MyriadPro-Regular",
    "font size" : 7.0,
    "text color" : "[0.75, 0.68, 0.67, 0.902]",
    "content" : "Figure 15.5.10 Detection of genotoxicity and cytotoxicity in valinomycin-treated A549 cells using the HCS DNA Damage Kit (H10292). A549 cells were treated with 30 µM or 120 µM valinomycin for 24 hr before performing the assay. With increasing concentrations of valinomycin, cells showed genotoxic e ects as indicated by detection with a pH2AX antibody in conjunction with Alexa Fluor® 555 goat anti–mouse IgG antibody (orange  uorescence), and cytotoxic e ects as indicated by staining with the Image-iT® DEAD Green™ viability stain (green  uorescence). Blue- uorescent Hoechst 33342 was used as a nuclear segmentation tool, and Alexa Fluor® 647 phalloidin was used to visualize F-actin (pseudocolored magenta). The image on the left shows untreated cells with intact F-actin cytoskeletons and no evidence of cytotoxicity or genotoxicity. The image on the right shows cells treated with 120 µM valinomycin, which completely disrupted the actin cytoskeletons, increased levels of DNA damage and compromised plasma membrane integrity."
  }, {
